 Short introduction to object-oriented concepts of Fortran 90
===================================================================

 This project is still under development, so files/routines/modules
 are supposed to change frequently.

 To include these fortran 90 features, use Fortran90/95 compiler. 
 Type first './pamadm --adm --emb --oof', then 'make'.

 If you change the content of some module, run first 'make mod' to create the refreshed
 "*.mod" file.

 What is in the directory oof/
===============================
Two files: "tests.F" with callings of some F90 modularized routines from "routines.F".

Module subroutines contain encapsulated "older" F77 DIRAC routines, what is the main
intent - to "hide" into modules/derived data types as much as possible, employ new F90
routines to handle this data.

Memmory handling
================

Still using DIRAC 'big array' concept. First call of "Stick_into_array" sticks into the array
through the "memmory" module. The "PW" pointer becames associated with the big WORK array.


Quaternion matrixes
====================

Call of "Allocate_QMATRIX" (constructor from module "QMatrixes") allocates space for
a simple quaternion matrix.
"MakeUnitQMATRIX" makes it a unit matrix, while "PrintQMATRIX" prints the matrix out.

"DeAllocate_QMATRIX" releases part of WORK array.

Further development
===================

Everyone is welcome to extent functionalities of modules. How about matrix multiplication
(as operator overloading), definition of vector data types, extension of qmatrixes for
storing symmetry adapted quantities.





